WEBVTT

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.570
<i>Your summer camp starts today? 
Where are you going again?</i>

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:19.040
<i>The Geological Museum and JAXA! 
Endou-sensei was the one who proposed it!</i>

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.000
<i>Wow, sounds nice. 
Have enough fun for me, too!</i>

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:25.820
<i>Also, sneak pictures of 
everyone's sleeping faces!</i>

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:30.860
Look, a rocket!

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:35.340
That's the Expo Center's life-size model.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.580
Do you know this area well?

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:39.650
Yeah.

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:41.470
I lived here in my high school days.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:42.680
Hey, what's that?

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:44.520
That car's like a robot!

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:48.170
Be quiet! You made me miss our turn!

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:22.700
04. Exciting! Summer Camp!

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.700
We're here!

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:25.090
You're restless for once.

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:26.900
I'm not acting any different.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:29.350
I see rock samples in books 
all the time, anyway.

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:31.970
Senpai, Senpai, look at this! It's incredible!

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:32.960
What is it?

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:35.090
A molted praying mantis shell!

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:36.800
Oh, how cute!

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:38.300
Ow, ow!

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:40.160
Darn you people!

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:40.160
What did I do?!

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.090
Good grief.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:47.100
Earth Sciences Club
 Summer Camp

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:47.100
JAXA 
Space Center

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:47.100
Geological
 Museum

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:47.100
Botanical 
Garden

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:47.100
Endou-sensei has a nice 
proposal for our summer camp.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:50.770
Want to go to my grandma's place?

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:53.730
Wow, those faces...

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:59.360
Grandma's Place

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:59.360
Geological 
Museum

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:59.360
Geopark

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:56.080
What I mean is, why don't we use her home

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.360
to avoid lodging expenses, 
and go visit the places nearby?

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:00.820
Places nearby?

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:05.030
Earth Sciences Club
Summer Camp

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:05.030
JAXA
 Space Center

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:05.030
Geological
 Museum

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:05.030
Geopark

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:05.030
Botanical 
Garden

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:07.640
There's the JAXA Space Center, 
the Geological Museum, Geopark, and...

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:09.540
I guess that settles it?

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:11.500
Fascinating Minerals

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:15.100
Wow, there are so many stones!

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:23.060
There are minerals and fossils. 
It's incredible, isn't it, Sakura-ch—

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:26.180
This is the actual stone 
used in reference books!

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:27.810
Ino-cha—

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:32.050
I'm having so much fun because
 Sakura-senpai's enjoying herself so much!

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:34.180
Let's walk at a bit of a distance.

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:35.600
Yeah.

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:40.720
Did you find something interesting?

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:41.570
This one.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:45.070
Wow, there's sparkly golden 
dice growing from the rock!

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.280
This one's heart-shaped.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:51.160
I wonder how they end up 
in such pretty shapes.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:55.250
It's Yamanashi's prefectural stone?

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:55.250
<b>Yamanashi Prefectural Stone (Mineral)</b>

00:03:55.490 --> 00:04:00.260
The stone that represents each region 
is recognized as that prefecture's stone.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:02.470
This one's my favorite!

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:04.430
<b>Kyoto Prefectural Stone (Mineral)</b>

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:04.430
<b>Cherry Blossom 
Stone</b>

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:04.430
<b>Cordierite-Muscovite
 Pseudomorph</b>

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:04.430
Cherry Blossom Stone?

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:05.570
Yes!

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:09.330
It's Kyoto's stone, and it's a national treasure.

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:12.370
It really does have a pattern 
like a cherry blossom flower!

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:16.930
It's so cute and rare! It really 
is a prized flower beyond reach!

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:20.420
It's just like Sakura-senpai, and I love it!

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:21.070
Huh?

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:22.150
Huh?

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:25.490
The geology group's excitement is incredible.

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:27.870
What are you looking at, Sensei?

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:30.370
Oh, there are so many precious gems!

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:33.940
I wonder how much all of 
these are worth together...

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:34.830
Money...

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:37.580
Monroe-senpai, what's on display there?

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:39.590
It looks delicious!

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:41.010
Her appetite...

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:44.220
E-Everyone has their own 
way of enjoying things.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:10.680
This is incredible.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:12.940
I don't think I can see 
everything in a single day!

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:14.950
I wish we could stay here forever.

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:18.040
Mira, did you bring the stone?

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:19.490
Oh, yeah.

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:20.550
Stone?

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:23.950
There's a chance the mudstone Mira picked up

00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:27.680
during our riverside 
barbecue could be a fossil,

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:29.720
so I thought I'd have them appraise it here.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:32.430
When I tripped, the stone split open.

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:36.060
What is that?! Scary!

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:38.120
Fossil One...

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:40.810
Mira, you tried looking into
it on your own, didn't you?

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:43.320
I wasn't totally certain it was a fossil,

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:45.930
so I decided to do some 
research on my own first.

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:46.820
<b>Fun Fossils</b>

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:46.820
Wow.

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:49.110
This book is easy to understand!

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:50.240
Oh, you're right.

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:52.790
My, that looks fun to read.

00:05:52.790 --> 00:05:54.240
<i>A picture book...</i>

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:58.170
So the main factor is whether 
or not an animal or plant

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:01.210
gets buried in mud or sand 
quickly after it dies.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:05.320
If not, it gets eaten by 
other animals or rots away.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:05.990
Uh-huh.

00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:10.360
Then, after thousands of years of being pressed under the weight of the mud

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:12.160
while minerals seep into it, 
it becomes a fossil.

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:16.370
In the most recent experiments, they've 
found fossils that can form in a few weeks.

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:19.350
Meaning... if you buried me right now,
you could present me

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:21.620
as a fossil for this year's cultural festival!

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:22.690
Fossilized
 Mira

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:22.690
That'd be a serious crime.

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:24.630
That's surprising, though.

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:27.190
I can't believe we found
 a fossil in a river stone.

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:29.940
It's not that uncommon.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:33.580
There's a chance it could happen 
anywhere there are sedimentary rocks.

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:36.960
Wow! Did you find all of these, Sakura-chan?

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:40.840
I-I bought all but this one and 
this one from a science museum.

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:43.170
Sakura-senpai's too honest!

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:48.210
Anyway, there's a chance it could be a 
fossil that surfaced after thousands of years,

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:51.300
so I want to find out what it might be.

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:54.180
What's with that look?

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:56.130
Come on, it's time!

00:06:59.570 --> 00:07:00.850
Hmm...

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:03.830
I picked the stone up by the river.

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:07.190
What section of the river 
did you pick it up in?

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:09.650
Uh, around here.

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:11.610
Which means...

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:15.520
Um, is this a fossil of a leaf 
from thousands of years ago?!

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:17.240
Hey, quiet down.

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:21.460
That large black spot might not be a fossil.

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:22.420
Huh?

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:26.240
What?! But Sakura-senpai was
so confident when she said—

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:28.300
I said be quiet!

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:31.350
Have you ever heard of a "hidden stone"?

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:33.970
Hidden stone?

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:37.280
Yes, it's also known as dendrite.

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:42.640
<b>Pseudofossil</b>

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:42.640
It forms when manganese flows between 
rocks and forms a plantlike shape.

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:47.610
It's easy to mistake it for a fossil, 
so they're often known as pseudofossils.

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:49.820
A false fossil...

00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:52.320
Oh, but you know, look here.

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:55.320
Spicules

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:55.320
Fish Scale

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:55.320
This shiny spot, about 3 millimeters wide.

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:58.000
This is from a fish's scale, I think.

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:02.110
Also, it's tiny and hard to see,

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:05.880
but can you see the tiny 
lines just past the label?

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:08.090
Let's take a look with this.

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:09.940
I see them!

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:13.410
Those are likely the spicules of sea sponges.

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:18.140
It's probably the skeletal structure of a sea 
creature from the end of the Precambrian Era.

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:21.820
It's very small, but it's a real fossil.

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:22.850
A fossil!

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:25.270
A fossil!

00:08:26.130 --> 00:08:28.070
A fossil!

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:29.780
A fossil! A fossil!

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:36.620
Here you go. This explains the geological map 
of the area where you found the stone.

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:38.550
Thank you!

00:08:39.290 --> 00:08:41.480
The names and history of the strata...

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:43.710
Even the commonly found fossils 
are described in here.

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:48.090
You can view those records online, 
too. Do you want to see how?

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:49.250
Yeah!

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:57.090
The geology group feel asleep.

00:08:57.090 --> 00:08:58.970
There was a lot of information for them.

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:02.020
Sakura-chan was especially excited today.

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:05.690
Yeah, just like a little kid.
She was even cuter than usual.

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:09.140
Sakura-senpai!

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:10.390
She's not waking up at all.

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:15.110
You're not a child, 
so please walk on your own!

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:18.140
Oh, it's Yuki and her students.

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:19.150
Welcome.

00:09:19.150 --> 00:09:20.580
It was a long trip, wasn't it?

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:22.090
I'm home.

00:09:22.090 --> 00:09:23.450
Thank you for having us.

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:25.910
Mira, that old man's hat...

00:09:25.910 --> 00:09:27.780
Thank you for having us.

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:27.600
Huh? JAXA?

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:29.670
Is her grandfather an employee there?

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:29.670
You're welcome.

00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:33.340
The logo's shaped a bit different, though.

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:36.090
It's JA's!

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:36.090
The farmers' cooperative!

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:39.290
He's a space-loving farmer.

00:09:39.290 --> 00:09:41.320
Please go easy on him.

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:49.020
<b><i>Requesting information!</i></b>

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:49.020
<b>We're looking for asteroids</b>

00:09:47.590 --> 00:09:49.020
Hello!

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:51.490
We're Hoshizaki High School's 
earth sciences club!

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:52.710
What's that?

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:55.020
W-We're searching for an asteroid.

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:56.150
A strategy those two came up 
with before our summer camp.

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:58.030
We're Hoshizaki High School's 
earth sciences club!

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:00.370
Doing that at the space center just 
makes it look like they're fooling around.

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:02.660
Oh, but someone's talking to them.

00:10:04.670 --> 00:10:06.120
We got candy!

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:09.770
Sorry for the wait.

00:10:09.770 --> 00:10:12.790
So, to take the observation tour, 
we need to meet up at...

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:15.010
Grandpa
Missed Call

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:15.010
Grandpa...

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:18.300
Your grandfather looked like 
he really wanted to come.

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:20.590
Are you sure we shouldn't have brought him?

00:10:20.590 --> 00:10:21.220
Huh?

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:23.490
Oh, it's fine, it's fine.

00:10:23.490 --> 00:10:25.430
This is your summer camp, after all.

00:10:26.030 --> 00:10:27.180
A rocket!

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:29.760
What you saw yesterday was a model,

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:32.970
but this is a real rocket, even on the inside.

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:33.820
A real one?!

00:10:34.050 --> 00:10:36.150
I want to touch it!

00:10:36.150 --> 00:10:39.520
Hey, Monroe, your astronomy 
group is getting carried away.

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:42.460
As our president, you need to watch... them...

00:10:43.870 --> 00:10:45.790
I can't reach it!

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:47.670
Space is so far away!

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:52.540
They all get along so well.

00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:54.960
Yeah.

00:10:55.870 --> 00:10:57.410
This is incredible, Ao!

00:10:57.410 --> 00:10:58.930
It's a space center!

00:10:58.930 --> 00:11:01.890
Maybe we'll find a hint to 
discovering our asteroid!

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:02.680
Yeah.

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:05.490
Are you two interested in asteroids?

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:09.400
Come on, Sensei! We told you about 
it the day we joined this club!

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:11.690
Huh? Oh, you did?

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:14.380
Senpai, let's take a commemorative photo!

00:11:15.290 --> 00:11:16.710
I see.

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:20.950
Thank you all for participating 
in the observation tour.

00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:23.950
Let's get started by watching 
this introduction video.

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:31.790
<i>This is the place closest 
to space in all of Japan.</i>

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:34.690
<i>Welcome to the space center.</i>

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:40.080
<i>Here we'll introduce JAXA's various projects.</i>

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:47.120
Space is incredible! Mankind is incredible!

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:48.610
Huh? Mira-chan?

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:50.770
I don't think that video was 
meant to be cried over...

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:54.230
Getting older sucks.

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:57.230
Are we the minority here?

00:11:57.230 --> 00:12:01.360
Now I'll show you all to 
the astronaut training wing.

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:04.060
Normally, only staff are 
allowed into this area,

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:05.750
Monroe-senpai's looking 
more serious than usual.

00:12:04.060 --> 00:12:08.730
so please attach the passes 
I gave you somewhere visible.

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:08.730
Well, I'm sure she has a lot of 
thoughts running through her mind.

00:12:09.220 --> 00:12:11.040
She's always wanted to come here.

00:12:11.270 --> 00:12:12.510
She has?

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:17.170
The space suits here weigh 
roughly 120 kilograms.

00:12:17.170 --> 00:12:20.820
They include life support, 
oxygen, and batteries.

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:22.880
Also, when they're working 
outside the ship...

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:26.930
On the other side of this window is 
the closed-environment training facility.

00:12:26.930 --> 00:12:31.880
The previous exams narrowed our 
963 applicants down to the ten

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:33.870
who are taking their tests in here.

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:37.710
Um, what does it take 
to become an astronaut?

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:42.160
Well, first, you have to be healthy,

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:43.900
and also proficient in English.

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:46.930
And above all else,
 you need to be cooperative.

00:12:46.930 --> 00:12:49.240
That's said to be the most important part.

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:50.970
Thank you.

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:52.050
No problem.

00:12:52.050 --> 00:12:55.780
Over here, we place astronauts in 
low-pressure environments to measure

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:54.010
You heard her.

00:12:54.010 --> 00:12:55.210
Sakura-chan.

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:57.950
Thank you very much.

00:12:55.780 --> 00:13:00.920
how low oxygen supplies will affect their 
bodies and conduct other training regimes.

00:12:57.950 --> 00:13:00.920
You'll need to work more
 on your pronunciation.

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:05.220
We should try asking 
her about asteroids, too!

00:13:06.590 --> 00:13:08.600
Asteroids?

00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:09.830
Yeah!

00:13:09.830 --> 00:13:12.480
We were hoping for a hint 
on how to discover one.

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:16.340
I'm just a tour guide, so I don't 
know very much about that...

00:13:16.340 --> 00:13:20.110
but one of the exhibit hall's 
commentators might know something.

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:21.970
I see.

00:13:21.970 --> 00:13:22.960
I'm sorry.

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:25.320
It's okay. Thank you for the advice.

00:13:25.960 --> 00:13:29.030
All right! The enemy waits 
in the exhibit hall!

00:13:29.030 --> 00:13:30.420
Let's go, Ao!

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:31.220
Yeah!

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:37.460
It's a manmade satellite!

00:13:51.790 --> 00:13:53.750
It's an exhibit about the Hayabusa.

00:13:53.750 --> 00:13:55.140
I'm sure someone here...

00:13:55.780 --> 00:13:58.210
U-Um, excuse me!

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:02.440
You want to discover an asteroid?

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:05.420
D-Do you have any hints for us?

00:14:05.420 --> 00:14:06.820
Any hints, eh?

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:10.820
Or do you know anyone who might 
be an expert in that field?

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:17.460
Well, this center is focused 
on space development,

00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:19.950
so while we have some exhibits 
on the discovery of asteroids,

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:22.590
we don't know much about discovering 
new astronomical objects.

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:27.050
There might be a specialist in one of our other 
centers elsewhere in the country, but...

00:14:27.050 --> 00:14:28.700
I see.

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:30.070
Thank you.

00:14:30.070 --> 00:14:31.250
Thank you.

00:14:31.250 --> 00:14:32.180
Not at all.

00:14:32.490 --> 00:14:34.330
Thank you very much!

00:14:34.330 --> 00:14:36.720
I couldn't find any clues.

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:37.700
Ao...

00:14:37.700 --> 00:14:42.720
Me, neither. But I learned a lot today,
and I did find out some things.

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:43.770
For example,

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:48.960
if we try each of the centers throughout Japan, 
we'll eventually meet an expert on it!

00:14:50.610 --> 00:14:52.390
You're too optimistic.

00:14:52.390 --> 00:14:54.810
Sakura-chan, thanks for your help.

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:56.870
Honestly, I couldn't just watch you girls...

00:14:57.370 --> 00:14:59.800
And you should be helping, too!

00:14:58.340 --> 00:14:59.800
Yay!

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:01.330
Where's your cooperation?!

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:04.640
Sensei, we're finished looking around!

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:06.300
Oh, good to hear.

00:15:06.590 --> 00:15:08.080
Look, space meals!

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:09.760
They sell all different kinds!

00:15:10.220 --> 00:15:12.590
A replica space suit!

00:15:12.860 --> 00:15:14.970
Sensei, what did you buy?

00:15:15.720 --> 00:15:17.680
That's what I want to ask!

00:15:18.430 --> 00:15:21.140
Well, we should be heading back.

00:15:21.370 --> 00:15:23.100
Where's Monroe?

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:25.440
She went to use the restroom.

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:39.060
You sure took a long time.

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:40.580
What's that look for?

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:42.590
Space is so far away!

00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:43.260
Huh?

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:47.060
Oh, welcome home.

00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:48.820
Everything's ready for the barbecue.

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:51.260
Wow! It's like a festival!

00:15:51.560 --> 00:15:55.220
I put some effort into it, 
since I had a lot of free time.

00:15:55.220 --> 00:15:56.500
Oh, jeez...

00:15:56.500 --> 00:15:58.600
I'm sorry I didn't bring you with us, okay?

00:15:59.140 --> 00:16:00.810
You can have this as an apology.

00:16:01.030 --> 00:16:03.270
Y-Yuki!

00:16:07.910 --> 00:16:09.920
I guess tonight's a full moon.

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:14.030
Yeah, it is. That makes it 
harder to see the stars.

00:16:14.240 --> 00:16:17.970
Nights like that are good 
for viewing the moon, though.

00:16:17.970 --> 00:16:20.840
Shall I break out the telescope, then?

00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:23.930
Can you students help me with that?

00:16:23.930 --> 00:16:25.380
Sure!

00:16:26.200 --> 00:16:28.670
Oh, it has an equatorial mount.

00:16:28.670 --> 00:16:31.380
It has different parts than 
the one our club uses.

00:16:31.810 --> 00:16:36.720
The mount on ours is an alt-azimuth mount that 
moves the telescope vertically and horizontally.

00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:40.890
This one uses an equatorial mount that 
moves in time with the earth's rotation.

00:16:41.440 --> 00:16:44.110
Once you move the handle 
on an equatorial mount,

00:16:44.110 --> 00:16:46.440
it'll keep following that star forever, 
so it's much easier!

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:47.870
Yay!

00:16:47.870 --> 00:16:49.770
I want to see, too!

00:16:50.920 --> 00:16:54.740
I thought the moon was more bumpy.

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:57.290
It's surprisingly flat.

00:16:57.290 --> 00:17:02.930
Oh, that's because light from the sun reflects
off the surface during the full moon,

00:17:02.930 --> 00:17:05.540
so it's harder to see the 
bumps from the craters.

00:17:05.540 --> 00:17:10.360
By the way, the bright part with lots 
of craters is mostly anorthosite,

00:17:10.360 --> 00:17:12.940
and the dark parts are made of basalt.

00:17:12.940 --> 00:17:15.090
Wow, really?

00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:18.280
Oh, she's a little relieved.

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:20.490
You studied up on it, didn't you?

00:17:21.100 --> 00:17:24.340
You should all study up on rocks a bit more!

00:17:24.340 --> 00:17:26.390
You're taking it out on us?!

00:17:26.620 --> 00:17:28.900
Hey, we have a watermelon!

00:17:28.900 --> 00:17:30.190
Watermelon?!

00:17:30.820 --> 00:17:35.610
Oh, right. Here's what you 
asked us to look for, Yuki.

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:38.320
Oh, you found it? Thanks.

00:17:38.570 --> 00:17:40.670
An old flyer?

00:17:40.670 --> 00:17:44.790
For high schoolers... An astronomical 
object discovery program?

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:46.750
<b>Shining Star
Challenge</b>

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:46.750
<b>Recruiting Now</b>

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:46.750
Huh? What is this?!

00:17:46.750 --> 00:17:51.620
It's the Shining Star Challenge program held 
at the observatory during summer break.

00:17:52.690 --> 00:17:55.210
I participated in it myself back in the day.

00:17:55.210 --> 00:17:57.430
I couldn't find anything, though.

00:17:57.430 --> 00:18:02.440
Wh-Wh-Why would you keep such 
incredible information a secret?!

00:18:02.440 --> 00:18:05.390
Well, because I didn't know about your dream.

00:18:05.770 --> 00:18:08.470
Huh? But when we first joined the club...

00:18:08.470 --> 00:18:11.360
Sorry. I was sleeping in the next room over.

00:18:11.600 --> 00:18:12.680
You slept through it...

00:18:13.220 --> 00:18:17.640
Oh, the deadline for entering 
this year has already passed.

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:18.110
New Astronomical Object Discovery 
Program for High Schoolers
Shining Star Challenge

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:18.110
Applications for this event are closed.

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:18.110
Add to list

00:18:18.110 --> 00:18:21.310
If only we had known about this sooner!

00:18:19.700 --> 00:18:21.980
It's held on Ishigaki Island.

00:18:21.310 --> 00:18:23.950
You're mad, aren't you?!

00:18:21.980 --> 00:18:23.950
Okinawa?! That's great!

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:26.910
Ishigaki Island...

00:18:27.330 --> 00:18:28.370
Okinawa...

00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:30.170
Blue seas...

00:18:30.170 --> 00:18:31.300
Mira?

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:33.800
The Shining Star Challenge.

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:36.510
It's open to all high 
schoolers in the nation.

00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:39.460
Seven participants are selected 
based on their submitted assignment.

00:18:39.460 --> 00:18:43.900
It's hard to get in, and there's no 
guarantee we'll discover one, huh?

00:18:43.900 --> 00:18:45.840
But I still feel

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:48.740
like we're a step or two closer to our goal.

00:18:48.740 --> 00:18:49.480
Yeah.

00:18:49.850 --> 00:18:52.550
This might be a huge leap forward for us.

00:18:52.550 --> 00:18:53.360
Yeah!

00:18:56.640 --> 00:18:58.730
This brings back memories.

00:18:58.730 --> 00:19:01.020
Your eyes sparkled just like theirs

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:04.120
when you came home from Ishigaki Island.

00:19:09.050 --> 00:19:13.000
Touring JAXA, having a barbecue, 
viewing the stars...

00:19:13.350 --> 00:19:14.980
and ending with fireworks.

00:19:14.980 --> 00:19:16.890
We've been going all-out.

00:19:16.890 --> 00:19:21.610
I never expected Endou-sensei to be someone 
who hunted for new asteroids, though.

00:19:21.610 --> 00:19:23.570
I never knew that, either.

00:19:23.570 --> 00:19:26.260
My heart's been racing all day long.

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:31.230
I guess this means the astronomy 
group's gotten closer to their goals.

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:32.730
Goals?

00:19:33.100 --> 00:19:37.070
I want to become an astronaut.

00:19:37.070 --> 00:19:38.760
An astronaut?!

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:39.780
Really?!

00:19:40.070 --> 00:19:40.940
Yeah.

00:19:40.940 --> 00:19:43.940
So that's why you were 
acting so serious today.

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:47.490
It's such a big dream, 
it's really a bit embarrassing.

00:19:48.230 --> 00:19:50.570
You shouldn't call it embarrassing.

00:19:50.960 --> 00:19:52.750
Not if you're serious about pursuing it.

00:19:54.020 --> 00:19:57.090
I still haven't found any solid 
goal to pursue, so if you ask me,

00:19:57.600 --> 00:20:00.100
you're all amazing for having one.

00:20:00.690 --> 00:20:03.900
You shouldn't hold back 
in pursuit of your dreams.

00:20:05.350 --> 00:20:07.700
Sakura-chan, thank you.

00:20:07.700 --> 00:20:08.470
Hmph.

00:20:08.930 --> 00:20:10.880
Uh, meaning...

00:20:10.880 --> 00:20:13.350
You're not mad at us?

00:20:13.650 --> 00:20:15.980
Huh? I'm giving you lots of encouragement.

00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:17.860
<i>I-It was hard to tell...</i>

00:20:18.350 --> 00:20:21.070
Don't hold back on your dreams...

00:20:22.470 --> 00:20:26.760
I think I might have figured 
out what I want to do.

00:20:27.870 --> 00:20:30.540
The truth is, there's somewhere 
I'd like to go, too!

00:20:31.910 --> 00:20:36.220
This is the headquarters of map-making, 
the Geospatial Informational Authority of Japan!

00:20:38.350 --> 00:20:42.050
I didn't know Inose was into this field.

00:20:42.300 --> 00:20:45.490
I know this is a change of plans. 
Are you all okay with this?

00:20:45.490 --> 00:20:47.460
I don't mind at all.

00:20:47.460 --> 00:20:48.300
Besides...

00:20:48.940 --> 00:20:53.520
I don't think anyone can complain 
when they see that look on her face.

00:20:55.980 --> 00:20:57.690
<b>Tsukuba VLBI Antenna</b>

00:20:58.620 --> 00:21:01.780
No wonder it felt a little lonesome.

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:04.350
What's the matter, Sensei?

00:21:04.350 --> 00:21:10.680
Hmm? Oh, there used to be this huge 
parabolic antenna around there.

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:25.880
Sensei!

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:28.140
We found the antenna!

00:21:28.640 --> 00:21:32.450
I think I've gone from feeling 
nostalgic to feeling happy.

00:21:35.100 --> 00:21:40.110
<i>We managed to discover new sides to 
our clubmates at this summer camp.</i>

00:21:40.880 --> 00:21:43.140
<i>We learned so much during our tours, too,</i>

00:21:43.140 --> 00:21:45.900
<i>and it feels like the world's 
opened up for everyone.</i>

00:21:47.310 --> 00:21:52.060
<i>Before, our goal of discovering 
an asteroid felt vague...</i>

00:21:52.570 --> 00:21:54.180
<i>But I'm sure there's a star out there</i>

00:21:54.180 --> 00:21:58.100
<i>just like the tiny fossil 
I discovered by the riverside.</i>

00:21:58.920 --> 00:22:00.870
<i>Somewhere out there in space.</i>

00:23:35.010 --> 00:23:40.060
05.
Everyone's Summer Break

00:23:35.990 --> 00:23:39.090
Next time, "Everyone's Summer Break."
